  "notes": "West leads the ♠J against 3NT. There are 7 top tricks (3 spades, 2 hearts, and 2 diamonds). An eighth trick can be developed in clubs after the ♣A is dislodged. A ninth trick could come from a 3-3 break in either hearts, diamonds, or clubs. The presence of the ♣10 gives South additional chances by playing on that suit.\n\nIf South wins the opening lead in the closed hand and plays a club to the ♣Q, East may be able to win the ♣A and return a spade to dummy's ♠A.\n\nOn the second round of clubs, when East follows low, South will have a problem. Playing the ♣10 will lose when West began with a doubleton ♣J. Playing the ♣K will lose when West started with a small doubleton in clubs.\n\nSouth might also consider winning with the ♠A at trick 1 and playing a club towards the closed hand. When East follows low, South faces the same problem. Playing the ♣K loses when East started with 4 clubs to the ♣A and ♣J. Playing the ♣10 loses when West began with 2 or fewer clubs to the ♣J.\n\nSouth can resolve this problem by winning the opening lead in hand and leading the ♣K. The defense will win the ♣A (ducking cannot help) and play a spade to dummy's ♠A.\n\nSouth continues by cashing the ♣Q. If the ♣J drops, South has 9 tricks. If both defenders follow small to the second round of clubs, South can lead towards his ♣10 and succeed whenever East holds the ♣J.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠J against 3NT. There are 7 top tricks (3 spades, 2 hearts, and 2 diamonds). An eighth trick can be developed in clubs after the ♣A is dislodged. A ninth trick could come from a 3-3 break in either hearts, diamonds, or clubs. The presence of the ♣10 gives South additional chances by playing on that suit.",
    "If South wins the opening lead in the closed hand and plays a club to the ♣Q, East may be able to win the ♣A and return a spade to dummy's ♠A.",
    "On the second round of clubs, when East follows low, South will have a problem. Playing the ♣10 will lose when West began with a doubleton ♣J. Playing the ♣K will lose when West started with a small doubleton in clubs.",
    "South might also consider winning with the ♠A at trick 1 and playing a club towards the closed hand. When East follows low, South faces the same problem. Playing the ♣K loses when East started with 4 clubs to the ♣A and ♣J. Playing the ♣10 loses when West began with 2 or fewer clubs to the ♣J.",
    "South can resolve this problem by winning the opening lead in hand and leading the ♣K. The defense will win the ♣A (ducking cannot help) and play a spade to dummy's ♠A.",
    "South continues by cashing the ♣Q. If the ♣J drops, South has 9 tricks. If both defenders follow small to the second round of clubs, South can lead towards his ♣10 and succeed whenever East holds the ♣J."
  ],
